00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.040
Hello and welcome to this video in which we'll
look at the importance of sharing knowledge,

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.690
information, and performance measures with
other members of the team and other groups.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.460
Sharing information and knowledge can
increase the productivity of a team that

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.470
helps it to work faster and smarter.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.390
Sharing knowledge will generate improvement
ideas and ensure the best methods are

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.670
applied to work practices or processes.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.080
Let's take a look at these
three areas in more detail.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:33.045
Let's start by thinking back to teams
and their strengths and weaknesses.

00:00:33.585 --> 00:00:36.855
We learned that each individual within
a team has different skills that they

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:41.475
bring to the team and for good, productive
teams, these compliment each other.

00:00:42.015 --> 00:00:46.425
While the same applies to knowledge,
different people within an organisation or

00:00:46.425 --> 00:00:48.255
team bring with them their own knowledge.

00:00:48.825 --> 00:00:53.505
Some may know a little information for what's
needed, others may be experts, and some may

00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:55.425
not know anything for the particular topic.

00:00:57.305 --> 00:01:02.085
For example, imagine you are asked to join
a team to brainstorm where a new product

00:01:02.085 --> 00:01:04.185
hasn't been as successful as expected.

00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:08.595
On that team, some people will have
been involved in the initial idea

00:01:08.865 --> 00:01:10.395
and carried it though to deployment.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:14.895
Others may work in customer services
and be the ones receiving complaints.

00:01:15.465 --> 00:01:20.200
Other team members may not know much about the
product but have experience of problem solving.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.290
By sharing their experiences and knowledge,
the team can collaboratively work together

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.070
to understand what's gone wrong and
discuss solutions to improve the product.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.400
Information sharing.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.540
Let's begin by looking at how
information differs from knowledge.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.470
Knowledge that we've already looked
at is deeper than information.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.590
Knowledge draws on experience and expertise.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.280
It's the wisdom a person has from
knowing a lot about a subject.

00:01:48.045 --> 00:01:51.585
Information on the other hand is the
data or instructions that anyone can

00:01:51.585 --> 00:01:54.975
pick up from a sheet, as an example.

00:01:55.155 --> 00:01:56.505
Think about baking a cake.

00:01:57.104 --> 00:02:01.275
Anyone can get the information for how to
bake a cake from a recipe and instructions.

00:02:01.815 --> 00:02:05.085
It's only those with knowledge of
baking that know how to turn it into a

00:02:05.085 --> 00:02:07.365
successful cake that you can't wait to try.

00:02:08.195 --> 00:02:13.515
Within our team of problem solvers, customer
services can prepare information that's been

00:02:13.515 --> 00:02:16.105
gathered from customer complaints and feedback.

00:02:16.695 --> 00:02:20.565
This may be displayed in a graph,
survey or verbally passed on.

00:02:21.135 --> 00:02:24.645
Those with the knowledge of how and why
the product was developed can use this

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.745
information to understand what's gone wrong.

00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:30.765
By collaborating together, all members
of team can share their information

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.445
and knowledge to reach a solution.

00:02:34.335 --> 00:02:37.755
Performance measures allow an organization
to know if things are going right.

00:02:38.595 --> 00:02:42.114
They have targets that tell the
management if expectations are being met.

00:02:42.685 --> 00:02:47.894
In our example, they are used as a tool to know
if the new product is as successful as expected.

00:02:48.465 --> 00:02:52.575
By setting a target that a certain number
of units will be sold within, say the first

00:02:52.575 --> 00:02:57.825
three months, or that it will score a three
stars on TripAdvisor in the same timeframe,

00:02:58.185 --> 00:03:00.225
the company can monitor its success.

00:03:00.855 --> 00:03:06.584
So not only has the customer service department
received a lot of complaints, the product

00:03:06.584 --> 00:03:08.635
is also below its performance Measures.

00:03:09.405 --> 00:03:13.125
By bringing together the information
gathered by customer service and the

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:16.875
performance measures, the team can share
their knowledge to solve the initial product

00:03:16.875 --> 00:03:19.665
difficulties and develop a successful product.

